0001>. By the discussion following\nDefinition <REF_0002>\nthis means we can find\nmorphisms <MATH_0011> of <MATH_0012> lying over\nclosed immersions <MATH_0013>\nwhere <MATH_0014> are Artinian local rings with residue field <MATH_0015>,\nan <MATH_0016> and a commutative diagram\n<MATHSEG_0001>lying over<MATHSEG_0002>\nsuch that there does <TEXTBFOPEN_0001>not<TEXTBFCLOSE_0001> exist an <MATH_0017> and a commutative diagram\n<MATHSEG_0003>lying over<MATHSEG_0004>\nWe may moreover assume that <MATH_0018> is a small\nextension, i.e., that the kernel <MATH_0019> of the surjection <MATH_0020>\nis isomorphic to <MATH_0021> as an <MATH_0022>-module.\nThis follows from Formal Deformation Theory, Remark\n<REF_0003>.\nThen we simply define\n<MATH_0023>\nBy property (RS*) we find <MATH_0024> over <MATH_0025> whose restriction to\n<MATH_0026> is <MATH_0027> and whose restriction to <MATH_0028> is <MATH_0029>\n(this gives the arrow <MATH_0030> lying over <MATH_0031>).\nTo finish the proof we verify conditions (1), (2), and (3).\n\n<STRUCT_0001><STRUCT_0002>\nBy the construction of the pushout we have a commutative diagram\n<MATH_0032>\nwith exact rows. This immediately proves (1) and (2).\nTo finish the proof we will argue by contradiction.\nAssume we have a pair <MATH_0033> as in (3).\nSince <MATH_0034> factors through <MATH_0035> we get the morphism\n<MATH_0036>\nSince <MATH_0037> is Artinian local with residue field <MATH_0038>\nwe see that <MATH_0039> lies over a morphism <MATH_0040>\nwhich factors through <MATH_0041>\nfor some <MATH_0042>. In other words, <MATH_0043> factors\nthrough <MATH_0044> giving a map <MATH_0045>.\nThe compatibility condition on the morphism <MATH_0046>\nin condition (3) translates into the condition that\n<MATH_0047>\nis commutative. This gives the contradiction we were looking for.\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:278476562af2a996cbc8243d4679228a81b190581b64b39a067e8bf2f96157ff", "unit_id": "tag:0G2J:proof"}
